 ],
 "literal": "Those who do not listen they will become, O friend, if they hear / Even if that which perishes is an excellent thing, on the bed strewn with flowers / your great beauty which was lost, / after your daily grief is removed, they will not delay indeed / On the lofty mountain slope where rustling bamboos rise high, / in the stone cave smelling of flesh where the tiger obtained its prey, / where people traveling on the path rest, / he who crossed the mountain of lofty peaks.",
 "translation": "O friend! If he hears of our state, he will not be one who does not listen. Even if your precious life itself should perish, once your daily grief is removed, he will not delay his return. He who crossed the mountain of lofty peaks, where rustling bamboos rise high on the slopes, and where travelers rest in the stone caves smelling of flesh where tigers have dragged their prey.",
 "modern": "தோழியே! நாம் சொல்வதைக் கேட்டால் அவர் கேளாதவர் ஆகமாட்டார். சிறந்ததாகிய நம் உயிர் அழிவதாக இருந்தாலும், உன்னுடைய தினசரித் துன்பம் நீங்குமாறு அவர் இனித் தாமதிக்க மாட்டார். ஒலிக்கின்ற மூங்கில்கள் உயர்ந்து வளர்ந்த ஓங்கிய மலைச்சாரலில், புலி தன் இரையைக் கொன்று தின்றதால் புலால் நாற்றம் வீசும் கற்குேகைகளில் வழிப்போக்கர்கள் தங்கிச் செல்லும் உயர்ந்த சிகரங்களைக் கொண்ட மலையைக் கடந்து சென்றவர் அவர்.",
 "explanation": "The friend (tōḻi) is speaking to the heroine (talaivi) to comfort her during the hero's absence in the desert (pālai) landscape. The heroine is wasting away with grief on her bed of flowers, losing her great beauty. The friend reassures her that if the hero only knew of her suffering, he would not delay his return, even if it meant abandoning his quest. She describes the perilous path he has taken—a mountain with high peaks and caves where tigers feast, yet safe enough for travelers to rest. This imagery suggests that though the journey is difficult, the hero is capable and will return safely to relieve her daily sorrow.",
 "model": "gemini-3.5-flash",
 "generated": "2026-11-26"
}